course: medicine
lesson: 2
title: The cast: itching, signing and the saw
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: A cast is a hard shell of plaster or fibreglass shaped round a broken arm so that the bone cannot move while it heals.
explain: It itches because the skin underneath is sweaty and old skin cells cannot flake off; the itch is a nuisance, not a sign of trouble.
explain: Never push a pencil or ruler inside: it can scratch the skin and start an infection nobody can see.
explain: The scary saw that takes it off does not spin; its blade wobbles fast back and forth, which cuts hard plaster but only jiggles soft skin.
explain: When the cast comes off, the arm looks thin, hairy and smelly, but muscle and skin come back with normal use and a bit of physio.
fact: A cast is a hard shell of plaster or fibreglass that stops a broken bone moving while it heals.
fact: Skin under a cast itches because it is sweaty and dead skin cells cannot flake off.
fact: Pushing anything inside a cast can scratch the skin, cause a hidden infection, or get stuck.
fact: Writing and drawing on the outside of a cast does not hurt the bone.
fact: A plaster cast that gets soaked goes soft and weak, and the damp padding can make the skin sore, so the fracture clinic should be phoned.
fact: An arm keeps swelling for a day or two after a break, and a tight cast can squeeze blood vessels and nerves.
fact: Fingers that go blue, white, cold or numb, or pain that gets much worse in a cast, need a doctor straight away.
fact: The cast saw's blade wobbles quickly back and forth instead of spinning, so it cuts plaster but not skin; on the arm it just feels warm and buzzy, though it sounds loud.
fact: After weeks in a cast the muscles are smaller and the joints stiff, and they recover over a few weeks.
fact: A physiotherapist gives exercises to bend and strengthen the arm a bit more each week; doing extra does not speed healing and can strain the healing arm.
fact: A sling holds the arm up against the body so it can rest and the swelling goes down.
fact: A just-healed bone is weaker than normal, so contact sports and trampolines wait a few weeks after the cast comes off.
fact: If your writing arm is in a cast, school can help with a scribe (someone who writes for you), a laptop or more speaking work.
fact: Some stable ankle and foot breaks, ones not likely to slip out of place, heal well in a removable boot instead of a cast.
exercise: What is a cast for? | Stopping the broken bone moving while it heals | Making the bone grow longer | Keeping germs off the arm | Showing everyone at school that you are hurt and need care
exercise: Why does skin itch under a cast? | It is sweaty and dead skin cannot flake off | The bone underneath is healing much too fast now | The plaster has poison in it that stings the skin | Tiny insects move in and live inside the cast
exercise: Why must you never push a pencil inside your cast? | It can scratch the skin and cause a hidden infection | It will break the bone again at once | Pencils make the cast heavier | The lead turns your skin grey for ever
exercise: Does drawing on the outside of a cast hurt the bone? | No, felt pens on the outside are fine | Yes, the ink soaks right through into the bone | Yes, the drawing makes the whole cast crack | No, but you must only ever draw in pencil
exercise: Your plaster cast got soaked in the bath. What should a grown-up do? | Ring the fracture clinic, as it may need replacing | Dry it on a hot radiator overnight, then it will be fine | Put it in the freezer to harden | Nothing, casts are waterproof
exercise: Your fingers go cold and blue in the cast. What does it mean? | The cast may be squeezing, so see a doctor straight away | Your fingers are just bored | The bone has healed early | Your cast is too loose
exercise: Why can the cast saw not cut your skin? | Its blade wobbles back and forth instead of spinning | It is made of soft rubber | It only works on Tuesdays | The nurse switches it off whenever it comes near your skin
exercise: Why is the arm thin when the cast comes off? | The muscles shrink when they are not used | The bone has dissolved | The cast squeezed the fat out | The arm has stopped growing for good and will stay small
exercise: What does a physiotherapist do after a break? | Gives exercises to make the arm bend and get strong again | Puts on a new cast every week | Takes more X-rays | Gives medicine to make bones grow
exercise: What does a sling do? | Holds the arm up so it rests and the swelling goes down | Keeps the arm warm | Pulls the bone straight | Makes the arm heal in just one week, so no cast is needed
exercise: You can't write with your broken writing arm. What might school do? | Give you a scribe, a laptop or more speaking work | Give you a detention | Make you write with your toes | Send you home for six weeks
exercise: Why no trampoline straight after the cast comes off? | The healed bone is still weaker and could break again | Trampolines make casts grow back | Your arm is too hairy to bounce | Doctors just do not like trampolines and ban them for everyone
exercise: Why might a doctor choose a boot instead of a cast? | The break is stable and a removable boot holds it still | The injury is not real | Boots heal bones faster than casts | Casts do not fit on ankles
exercise: Is the itch under a cast a sign something is wrong? | No, it is a normal nuisance | Yes, it means the bone is broken again | Yes, it means an infection for sure | No, it means the cast can come off
exercise: How does the cast saw feel on the skin? | Warm, buzzy and loud | Sharp and painful, like a knife | Freezing cold, like an ice cube | Like a bee sting on every bit
test: itches like mad mate, gonna stick my ruler down there. good idea? | No, it can scratch the skin and cause an infection | Yes, rulers are smooth so it's safe | Yes, as long as it's a wooden ruler and you go really slowly | No, use a knitting needle instead
test: Leo's cast got wet, so he plans to leave it on the radiator. What's wrong with that? | The damp padding stays wet inside and the cast is weakened | Radiators make casts too hard | Nothing, it will dry out fine | The cast will melt
test: Mia's fingers are numb and the pain is getting worse in her new cast. She says she'll be brave. Should she? | No, worse pain in a cast needs a doctor at once | Yes, being brave is always the answer | Yes, numbness means it is healing | No, she should cut the cast off herself with kitchen scissors
test: In a film, a man's cast is sawn off and the saw spins like a circular saw. What's wrong? | Cast saws wobble back and forth, not spin | Cast saws are made of paper | Casts are taken off with scissors | Nothing, cast saws really do spin very fast like that
test: My arm came out of the cast all thin and gross. Is it ruined for ever? | No, the muscle and skin come back with normal use | Yes, it will stay thin for life | Yes, unless you eat raw eggs | No, but the hair on it stays dark and thick for ever
test: Why does a physio give you just a few exercises and not loads? | Doing more than told can strain the healing arm | Exercises cost money each | Physios are lazy | More exercises would make the arm grow far too strong
test: I feel totally fine, so can I go back to rugby the day the cast comes off? | No, the bone stays weaker for a few weeks | Yes, feeling fine means you are fully healed | Yes, as long as you wear two shirts to pad it | No, you can never ever play rugby again after a break
test: Which part of the healing arm is in danger if the cast is too tight? | Blood vessels and nerves being squeezed | The cast cracking open from the inside out | The bone growing too long | The skin getting too warm to itch
test: Grace has a boot for her broken ankle, and Ollie says hers isn't a real break. Who is right? | Grace; a boot is just the right tool for that kind of break | Ollie; boots are only for sprains | Ollie; real breaks always need plaster | Neither; boots are for walking in snow
test: What would happen if a cast could be taken off every day like a boot for any break? | Unstable bones might slip out of place | The bone would heal much faster than before | Nothing at all would change for the bone | The arm would never itch, so it would be better
test: Is it rude to ask friends to sign your cast? | No, doctors don't mind writing on the outside | Yes, it damages the bone | Yes, only doctors and nurses are allowed to write on it | No, but only with a pencil
exam: State the principal reason for which a plaster cast is applied to a fractured limb. | To immobilise the bone throughout the period of healing | To accelerate the healing of the bone to a matter of days | To protect the limb from airborne germs | To indicate to others that the limb is injured
exam: It is reported that a patient's fingers have become cold, pale and numb beneath a newly applied cast. Identify the appropriate course of action. | Medical attention is to be sought without delay | The cast is to be warmed upon a radiator | The patient is to persevere until the following week | An implement is to be inserted to relieve the pressure
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the oscillating saw employed for the removal of casts? | Its blade vibrates rather than rotates and does not cut skin | Its blade rotates at very great speed | It is employed only under general anaesthetic | It severs both plaster and skin indiscriminately
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the limb upon removal of a cast. | Muscle wasting is temporary and resolves with normal use | Muscle wasting is permanent | The bone has reached its full strength | The limb shall cease to grow thereafter for the remainder of life
